  [1mResizing[0m [1mand[0m [1mSignals[0m

  When the terminal window changes size, the client sends the
  new dimensions to the server. The server resizes each affected
  pane's virtual screen, calls [38;5;153mioctl(TIOCSWINSZ)[39m on the PTY
  master, and the kernel delivers [38;5;153mSIGWINCH[39m to the foreground
  process group inside that pane. Programs like vim and less
  then redraw themselves. Because the server owns the resize
  logic, each pane can have a different size from its neighbors.

  [1mProcess[0m [1mManagement[0m [1mand[0m [1mCleanup

[0m  Each pane's shell is its own session leader with its own
  controlling PTY, so job control ([38;5;153mCtrl-z[39m, [38;5;153mfg[39m) works normally
  inside it. When a shell exits, the server notices the closed
  PTY, removes the pane, and rearranges the layout. If the last
  pane in a window closes, the window closes. If the last window
  in a session closes, the session ends, and when the server
  has no sessions left, it may exit.

  [1mWhy[0m [1mthe[0m [1mDesign[0m [1mMatters[0m

  The architecture trades a small amount of complexity for
  significant flexibility. The server holds the authoritative
  state, so clients are disposable. Emulating the terminal
  inside the multiplexer allows features such as scrollback
  search, synchronized panes, and reattachment to full-screen
  programs. The main costs are a second layer of escape-sequence
  handling, which occasionally disagrees with the outer
  terminal on newer features, and the need to pass through
  capabilities like true color and clipboard access carefully.
  Understanding the server, the PTY pairs, and the virtual
  screen explains nearly every behavior users notice, from why
  detaching keeps programs running to why a pane's colors may
  look slightly different from the window's.

