[38;5;231m[49m●[39m [1;3;4mClocks: How Humanity Learned to Measure Time[0m

  Every clock, from the sundial on a garden wall to the atomic
  standard in a national laboratory, answers the same question:
  how much time has passed? The answers have changed
  dramatically over the centuries, and each change reflects a
  deeper understanding of what time is and how it can be
  measured.

  [1mThe Earliest Timekeepers[0m

  The first clocks followed the sky. Shadows swept across
  sundials in ancient Egypt and Mesopotamia, and the length and
  direction of a shadow told people roughly what hour it was.
  Sundials had obvious limits. They were useless at night, on
  cloudy days, and at the poles in winter. Water clocks, or

